1
00:00:06,980 --> 00:01:41,340
 We've always wanted a voyage to the moon, what an adventure, going back in ancient times.

2
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:43,180
 Go again with these Greeks.

3
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:44,350
 Yes, you're right.

4
00:01:44,350 --> 00:01:47,360
 The Greeks dreamed of a tornado carrying a ship to the moon.

5
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:51,170
 Around the year 1000, the Persian king, Caicos, had another idea.

6
00:01:51,170 --> 00:01:52,770
 Go gather eagles.

7
00:01:52,770 --> 00:02:05,120
 These of law.

8
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:40,770
 169, Galileo observed the moon with a telescope.

9
00:02:40,770 --> 00:02:42,230
 Creators.

10
00:02:42,230 --> 00:02:43,290
 Mountains.

11
00:02:43,290 --> 00:02:53,120
 In 1700, the Englishman David Russon had an idea.

12
00:02:53,120 --> 00:03:58,220
 Detail for imaginary vocible. Why?

13
00:03:58,220 --> 00:04:01,920
 It wasn't powerful enough.

14
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:03,840
 They couldn't aim it accurately.

15
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:07,080
 It's just too far away.

16
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:08,700
 Yes, but there's another reason.

17
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:14,000
 Shooting a cannon is too strong. The projectile is too fast. The men inside would be crashed.

18
00:04:14,510 --> 00:04:21,110
 That is the precise answer. To date, in any known technology, there is only one way to send human beings into space safely, and that's rockets.

19
00:04:21,410 --> 00:04:24,930
 The first to achieve this was a Russian, Konstantin Chalovsky.

20
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:30,930
 The moon is 380,000 kilometers from the Earth and circles our planet every 27 days. You, the distance.

21
00:04:31,070 --> 00:04:31,610
 A long way.

22
00:04:32,180 --> 00:04:33,450
 Yeah, a long way.

23
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:34,920
 Anyway, you'll never get there.

24
00:04:35,660 --> 00:04:38,270
 I was listening to what I said. Konstantin?

25
00:04:38,270 --> 00:04:42,830
 You said it's 380,000 kilometers away, and so one day man will get there.

26
00:04:42,990 --> 00:04:45,150
 Regarding distance, you are right, Constantine.

27
00:04:45,330 --> 00:04:47,520
 But how would you suggest we get there?

28
00:04:47,620 --> 00:04:48,710
 Using a rocket, sir.

29
00:04:49,200 --> 00:05:03,380
 Jules Verne already wrote that hydrogen and hydrogen form an explosive mixture.

30
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:06,760
 There is combustion, and the hot gas is directed back away using a tube,

31
00:05:07,170 --> 00:05:08,650
 propelling the rocket forward.

32
00:05:10,870 --> 00:05:13,430
 He discovered everything.

33
00:05:13,670 --> 00:05:16,550
 He did the calculations, but it's always the same story.

34
00:05:16,630 --> 00:05:17,730
 No one was at all interested.

35
00:05:17,990 --> 00:05:23,660
 But just a few years later, an American, Robert Goddard...

36
00:05:23,660 --> 00:05:28,670
 That one didn't work, but I know what I'll do!

37
00:05:28,670 --> 00:05:51,870
 Oxygen and gasoline, that should work!

38
00:05:51,870 --> 00:05:53,370
 Right, let's try it!

39
00:05:53,370 --> 00:05:55,710
 This day is truly historic!

40
00:05:55,710 --> 00:05:58,850
 We are going to perform the first test of a liquid-powered rocket!

41
00:05:58,850 --> 00:06:01,550
 Only watch it, Robert, it might explode!

42
00:06:01,550 --> 00:06:23,640
 Liquid-fuel rockets wrote a new chapter in the story of astronautics.

43
00:06:23,640 --> 00:06:24,510
 Spaceflight.

44
00:06:24,510 --> 00:06:26,310
 But guarded remained almost unknown.

45
00:06:26,310 --> 00:06:27,520
 Meanwhile, in Europe...

46
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:39,470
 ...the test...

47
00:06:39,470 --> 00:06:43,760
 Von Braun, could you make a rocket?

48
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:46,510
 If in time, it's possible.

49
00:06:46,510 --> 00:07:01,930
 Then make one, and quick!

50
00:07:01,930 --> 00:07:26,910
 Range of more than 300 kilometers, a gyroscope, and a guidance system.

51
00:07:26,910 --> 00:08:18,160
 To see a terrible weapon, the Nazis lost the war.

52
00:08:18,160 --> 00:08:21,560
 After the war, it began. The race to the moon, the race to space.

53
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:24,990
 Of course, everyone needed the German rocket technicians.

54
00:08:24,990 --> 00:08:26,490
 We lost the war.

55
00:08:26,490 --> 00:08:30,210
 Just when our rockets with the 4,000-kilometer range were almost ready.

56
00:08:30,210 --> 00:08:31,310
 Simple.

57
00:08:31,310 --> 00:08:32,770
 The Russians are that way.

58
00:08:32,770 --> 00:08:34,720
 The Americans are that way.

59
00:08:34,720 --> 00:08:36,170
 Where do you want to go?

60
00:08:36,170 --> 00:08:38,750
 To Siberia or to Texas?

61
00:08:38,750 --> 00:08:40,690
 To Texas!

62
00:08:40,690 --> 00:08:42,780
 Oh, you prefer Siberia.

63
00:08:42,780 --> 00:08:44,280
 No, no, Tima, I made a mistake.

64
00:08:44,280 --> 00:08:45,920
 If you're to Texas, I wanna go to Texas!

65
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:54,830
 The great match would begin between the two most powerful nations in the world, the United States and the Soviet Union.

66
00:08:57,390 --> 00:09:00,710
 Nikita Khrushchev, champion of the Soviet Union.

67
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:06,550
 My right, the President of the United States, Dwight Eisenhower.

68
00:09:07,460 --> 00:09:08,480
 Khrushchev to serve.

69
00:09:17,450 --> 00:09:23,900
 The Soviets placed a satellite in orbit and it's circling the Earth.

70
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:25,050
 One, love.

71
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:31,930
 The Soviet Union puts Sputnik 2 in orbit with a dog aboard.

72
00:09:32,030 --> 00:09:33,490
 Two to nothing for the Soviet Union.

73
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:38,140
 Mr. President.

74
00:09:39,880 --> 00:09:43,750
 Flora, the first American satellite, is put in orbit.

75
00:09:44,110 --> 00:09:44,780
 Two to one.

76
00:09:46,850 --> 00:09:47,580
 Autovarist.

77
00:09:47,740 --> 00:10:01,880
 First pictures from the hidden side of the moon. Nice, aren't they?

78
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:07,320
 4-1 in favor of the Soviet Union. Khrushchev to serve.

79
00:10:08,430 --> 00:10:10,970
 I write the new American champion, John F. Kennedy.

80
00:10:11,940 --> 00:10:13,680
 7-2 in favor of the Soviet Union.

81
00:10:17,460 --> 00:10:24,560
 The Soviet Union. Gagarin and Titov, the first men in space orbit.

82
00:10:25,860 --> 00:10:28,750
 Two American launchings that do not achieve orbit.

83
00:10:29,910 --> 00:10:35,230
 Still, two points. 9-4 for the Soviet Union.

84
00:10:43,050 --> 00:10:48,790
 Is that all?

85
00:10:51,110 --> 00:10:57,440
 American astronauts in orbit and Mariner on its way to Venus.

86
00:10:59,890 --> 00:11:03,540
 The first woman in space is Russian, Valentina Tarechkova.

87
00:11:04,360 --> 00:11:07,540
 An American, Gordon Cooper, circles the Earth 22 times.

88
00:11:09,930 --> 00:11:12,970
 American champ, Lyndon Johnson.

89
00:11:16,530 --> 00:11:20,430
 What do you want, Comrade Leonid?

90
00:11:20,610 --> 00:11:23,110
 No, it's my play. I take your place.

91
00:11:23,190 --> 00:11:23,530
 Net!

92
00:11:23,790 --> 00:11:24,410
 Go away.

93
00:11:25,140 --> 00:11:28,910
 A new champion for the Soviet Union, Comrade Leonid Brezhnev.

94
00:11:37,580 --> 00:11:43,070
 Snapshots of the moon might interest you.

95
00:11:43,170 --> 00:11:45,770
 We took him from our satellite that's going around the moon.

96
00:11:46,750 --> 00:11:49,270
 The Soviet Leonov is the first to go for a spacewalk.

97
00:11:49,270 --> 00:11:52,830
 It lasted 10 minutes, and then an American, white, took one for 21 minutes.

98
00:11:53,570 --> 00:11:56,010
 The first round is won by the Soviet Union, 2114.

99
00:12:05,130 --> 00:12:07,810
 Merci beaucoup.

100
00:12:15,290 --> 00:12:17,600
 This is our first satellite in orbit.

101
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:27,230
 There were dozens of various satellites in orbit going around the Earth.

102
00:12:27,230 --> 00:12:31,140
 It had been sent to Venus, Mars, even to the Sun.

103
00:12:32,670 --> 00:12:35,710
 No one had ever walked on anything other than the planet Earth.

104
00:12:35,790 --> 00:12:36,510
 Not so far.

105
00:12:36,890 --> 00:12:39,990
 Neil Armstrong had been chosen for the American space program.

106
00:12:40,490 --> 00:12:44,170
 He will go down in history as a great explorer of the modern era.

107
00:12:57,350 --> 00:12:58,990
 We have to approach and then dock.

108
00:13:04,140 --> 00:13:05,020
 Preparing for docking, over.

109
00:13:06,860 --> 00:13:08,720
 Operation a success. We appear stable.

110
00:13:19,670 --> 00:13:20,650
 Engine fired on its own.

111
00:13:24,320 --> 00:13:27,770
 Round like a...

112
00:13:27,770 --> 00:13:29,120
 Have to disengage.

113
00:13:29,880 --> 00:13:32,710
 I've got to find a way or we won't make it.

114
00:13:42,190 --> 00:13:52,340
 Approaching the Pacific.

115
00:13:54,030 --> 00:13:54,730
 Shook up.

116
00:13:58,860 --> 00:13:59,880
 Turned Earth's atmosphere.

117
00:14:00,240 --> 00:14:01,840
 Getting very hot, but we're okay.

118
00:14:12,050 --> 00:14:24,000
 This space race match is becoming very exciting.

119
00:14:25,060 --> 00:14:26,580
 This game will be decisive.

120
00:14:28,280 --> 00:14:30,910
 Piece of the moon?

121
00:14:31,020 --> 00:14:31,940
 It's a promise, Mark.

122
00:14:40,430 --> 00:15:00,510
 Time for you to get up.

123
00:15:26,980 --> 00:15:34,500
 60 seconds and counting.

124
00:15:34,890 --> 00:15:36,240
 T-minus 50 seconds.

125
00:15:36,860 --> 00:15:38,240
 Heart rate, 75 per minute.

126
00:15:38,480 --> 00:15:40,120
 All systems are go and counting.

127
00:15:41,460 --> 00:15:42,220
 Astronauts ready.

128
00:15:42,360 --> 00:15:42,960
 10 seconds.

129
00:15:43,100 --> 00:15:47,660
 Nine, eight, seven, six, five, four, three, two, one, zero.

130
00:15:48,410 --> 00:15:48,690
 Mission.

131
00:16:15,760 --> 00:16:30,650
 Second station.

132
00:16:31,610 --> 00:16:32,360
 It's in Earth orbit.

133
00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:50,300
 Destination.

134
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:55,040
 In a weightless state.

135
00:17:01,960 --> 00:17:02,880
 Separate third stage.

136
00:17:04,930 --> 00:17:08,020
 The maneuver to turn us around.

137
00:17:08,610 --> 00:17:09,730
 Attaching the litter module.

138
00:17:13,010 --> 00:17:15,750
 Mission completed.

139
00:17:16,250 --> 00:17:17,790
 Eagle is attached to command.

140
00:17:19,560 --> 00:17:24,780
 Do something, the sun will roast us like so many chickens.

141
00:17:25,180 --> 00:17:26,410
 Give us a little spin.

142
00:17:27,370 --> 00:17:28,880
 Three rotations an hour.

143
00:17:34,970 --> 00:17:37,520
 Still, Neil, you're about to be a TV star.

144
00:17:42,840 --> 00:17:51,770
 Full star, Sirius Vega.

145
00:17:51,770 --> 00:17:55,770
 The computers taking our bearing were precisely 255,000 kilometers from the moon.

146
00:17:56,090 --> 00:17:57,020
 Let's sleep.

147
00:17:57,020 --> 00:17:59,580
 Look at that!

148
00:17:59,580 --> 00:18:09,600
 Accelerate.

149
00:18:09,600 --> 00:18:10,600
 Fifteen hundred kilometers.

150
00:18:10,600 --> 00:18:14,550
 You will be hidden from Earth over the hidden face of the moon.

151
00:18:14,550 --> 00:18:17,570
 Engine ignition to achieve lunar orbit.

152
00:18:17,570 --> 00:18:18,570
 Our speed?

153
00:18:18,570 --> 00:18:20,490
 Eight thousand kilometers an hour.

154
00:18:20,490 --> 00:18:21,550
 Seven thousand six.

155
00:18:21,550 --> 00:18:22,550
 Five thousand eight hundred.

156
00:18:22,550 --> 00:18:23,510
 You are in orbit.

157
00:18:23,510 --> 00:18:26,370
 Checking out systems on the Eagle.

158
00:18:26,370 --> 00:18:31,790
 Buzz, let's do it.

159
00:18:31,790 --> 00:18:37,900
 Systems are go.

160
00:18:37,900 --> 00:18:38,900
 We can separate lunar module.

161
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:40,900
 Ready to release in ten seconds.

162
00:18:40,900 --> 00:18:44,900
 Nine, eight, seven, six, five, four, three, two, one.

163
00:18:44,900 --> 00:18:45,900
 Releasing now.

164
00:18:45,900 --> 00:18:48,500
 It's free.

165
00:18:48,500 --> 00:18:51,170
 Eagle, do you read me? Eagle, do you copy?

166
00:18:51,430 --> 00:18:52,590
 Roger, loud and clear.

167
00:18:52,950 --> 00:18:53,970
 All systems are go.

168
00:18:54,530 --> 00:18:56,940
 The Eagle has wings, ready for moon landing.

169
00:18:57,900 --> 00:18:59,840
 Now is when the crucial decision has to be taken.

170
00:19:00,230 --> 00:19:02,430
 Then there was no going back, all or nothing.

171
00:19:03,360 --> 00:19:05,520
 It had to be perfect the first time, otherwise.

172
00:19:05,940 --> 00:19:06,820
 Otherwise what?

173
00:19:06,960 --> 00:19:08,100
 Otherwise they were lost.

174
00:19:08,500 --> 00:19:11,400
 They couldn't get back, not enough fuel to return to orbit.

175
00:19:11,870 --> 00:19:14,030
 Nobody could ever go and rescue them.

176
00:19:14,730 --> 00:19:16,070
 For them, it would be the end.

177
00:19:16,070 --> 00:19:19,990
 All systems go, activating engines to leave moon orbit, descending.

178
00:19:21,750 --> 00:19:40,640
 Zero two.

179
00:19:40,640 --> 00:19:43,690
 Saturated. Directing us toward a crater full of rocks.

180
00:19:43,690 --> 00:19:48,180
 The computer couldn't take any more data.

181
00:19:48,180 --> 00:19:50,180
 In a few seconds, it's moon landing.

182
00:19:50,180 --> 00:19:52,180
 Great boulders. The moon module could overturn.

183
00:19:52,180 --> 00:19:54,170
 A catastrophe.

184
00:19:54,170 --> 00:19:56,150
 Only one solution. To act quickly.

185
00:19:56,150 --> 00:19:58,910
 Now switching to manual control.

186
00:19:58,910 --> 00:20:01,320
 It's our only chance. Quick, find a landing site.

187
00:20:01,320 --> 00:20:03,440
 Looks good over there. Three degrees left.

188
00:20:03,440 --> 00:20:08,830
 Not over there, not too bad.

189
00:20:08,830 --> 00:20:11,020
 We'll go for it. Don't have much choice.

190
00:20:11,020 --> 00:20:16,600
 We'll run out of fuel.

191
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:22,860
 Sweat. Too much dust.

192
00:20:22,860 --> 00:20:25,860
 Can't see anything. We're going to land.

193
00:20:25,860 --> 00:20:26,820
 Touchdown, cut the motors.

194
00:20:28,330 --> 00:20:30,250
 Mission Control, Tranquility Base.

195
00:20:30,450 --> 00:20:31,410
 The Eagle has landed.

196
00:20:36,390 --> 00:20:37,150
 Sea of Tranquility.

197
00:20:37,520 --> 00:20:39,510
 We could use a little Tranquility right now.

198
00:20:39,630 --> 00:20:40,010
 Right, Collins?

199
00:20:40,330 --> 00:20:41,670
 More precision.

200
00:20:42,160 --> 00:20:45,360
 We don't know, I'm afraid.

201
00:20:45,600 --> 00:20:46,560
 You don't know where?

202
00:20:47,220 --> 00:20:48,870
 Mission Control calling Collins.

203
00:20:48,990 --> 00:20:49,550
 Do you read me?

204
00:20:50,010 --> 00:20:50,940
 Loud and clear.

205
00:20:51,470 --> 00:20:52,160
 The module.

206
00:20:52,430 --> 00:20:53,570
 Down on the moon, I guess.

207
00:20:53,790 --> 00:20:55,070
 But where, Collins, where?

208
00:20:55,220 --> 00:20:56,060
 I don't really know.

209
00:20:56,390 --> 00:20:57,530
 Stay in contact, Collins.

210
00:20:57,950 --> 00:21:00,090
 Negative, entering shadow area of the moon.

211
00:21:00,090 --> 00:21:02,770
 Come in, Collins.

212
00:21:02,950 --> 00:21:03,270
 Come in.

213
00:21:03,720 --> 00:21:04,400
 Come in.

214
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:08,260
 There's no point in trying.

215
00:21:08,360 --> 00:21:09,220
 He's behind the moon.

216
00:21:09,530 --> 00:21:11,310
 From there, no contact can be made.

217
00:21:11,350 --> 00:21:14,140
 We've lost them. They'll die on the moon, and they'll know they're going to die.

218
00:21:14,930 --> 00:21:16,480
 We'll find them. A lunar map.

219
00:21:22,020 --> 00:21:23,860
 Oh, wrong one. And not that one.

220
00:21:24,370 --> 00:21:26,590
 They're here. Collins will check it.

221
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:29,000
 Collins is still behind the moon.

222
00:21:29,460 --> 00:21:30,160
 To wait.

223
00:21:39,980 --> 00:21:44,070
 Me.

224
00:21:44,830 --> 00:21:47,760
 Roger, Collins. Loud and clear. And you?

225
00:21:48,050 --> 00:21:49,100
 Five by five.

226
00:21:49,810 --> 00:21:54,470
 Okay, we want you to check. We think they're in the West Crater about 500 meters in.

227
00:21:54,690 --> 00:21:55,560
 Are you sure?

228
00:21:56,080 --> 00:21:56,950
 Sure.

229
00:21:57,530 --> 00:21:59,060
 Okay, I'll take a look.

230
00:21:59,060 --> 00:22:02,540
 Guess we'll be needing a shave soon, huh?

231
00:22:03,600 --> 00:22:05,540
 Right, to work. Full checkup, okay?

232
00:22:08,350 --> 00:22:14,740
 Systems are go. Electronic circuits, pressure, oxygen, cooling system, TV transmitter.

233
00:22:15,310 --> 00:22:20,860
 We put on support system. We need these to survive on the moon.

234
00:22:21,340 --> 00:22:22,720
 So we can breathe.

235
00:22:24,760 --> 00:22:25,590
 Refrigeration, check.

236
00:22:25,630 --> 00:22:26,430
 Communication, check.

237
00:22:26,530 --> 00:22:27,290
 Ventilation, check.

238
00:22:27,990 --> 00:22:28,950
 Pressure, check.

239
00:22:29,640 --> 00:22:30,580
 Dehumidification, check.

240
00:22:30,700 --> 00:22:31,520
 Purification, check.

241
00:22:31,600 --> 00:22:33,140
 Okay, let's take a walk, all right?

242
00:22:37,280 --> 00:22:50,200
 And open the hat. We're all set.

243
00:22:50,510 --> 00:22:52,480
 Wait, just a second. Pressure's still too high.

244
00:22:54,270 --> 00:23:17,430
 This is it. You say you've got us watching at home.

245
00:23:17,430 --> 00:23:21,740
 Look, children, the moon, humanity's dream for millions of years, you realize.

246
00:23:22,060 --> 00:23:26,200
 And now, for the first time, a man will set foot on the surface of the moon.

247
00:23:31,990 --> 00:23:52,980
 Supposed to say, uh, one small step for a man, giant leap for mankind.

248
00:23:53,440 --> 00:23:55,340
 I guess I kind of like what he said there.

249
00:23:55,340 --> 00:23:59,530
 All over the world, hundreds of millions of people were glued to their TV screens.

250
00:24:01,180 --> 00:24:11,340
 Americans, Russians, Chinese, Indians, Eskimos, people in Africa, Puan, and the French, the Italians.

251
00:24:25,610 --> 00:24:29,540
 1901, the United States of America wins.

252
00:24:30,140 --> 00:24:31,840
 Were they able to come back?

253
00:24:31,840 --> 00:24:33,180
 Yeah, of course, safe and sound.

254
00:24:33,180 --> 00:24:34,850
 Did he bring any moon rocks back?

255
00:24:34,850 --> 00:24:35,970
 Many of them.

256
00:24:35,970 --> 00:24:38,300
 And the space race, wasn't it?

257
00:24:38,300 --> 00:24:40,140
 Well, the truth is, everybody did.

258
00:24:40,140 --> 00:24:44,670
 After the race to the moon, many countries decided to work together to pool their efforts

259
00:24:44,670 --> 00:24:47,190
 in the conquest of space, and it's going on today.

260
00:24:47,190 --> 00:24:50,750
 The astronauts of tomorrow, eh, maybe you'll be among them.

261
00:24:50,750 --> 00:25:00,830
 Des mondes meilleurs, des vagabonds tout ailleurs.

262
00:25:00,830 --> 00:25:09,110
 Voici que l'homme décolle, il va quitter le sol,

